{
  "dom_id": "step-q114245",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x]}^{\u2032}}{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{2x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1-x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{2x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x]}^{\u2032}}{{[2x-2]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
